problems.problem:
Prove that:

a) no prime can be represented as the sum of two or more consecutive odd positive integers;

b) the square of any prime can be represented as the sum of two or more consecutive odd positive integers in one and only one way.

On an infinite square grid, Jaak and Jüri play the following game:

Jaak has $n$ red counters and Jüri one blue counter, which at the beginning of the game are on different squares of the board;

Jaak's move consists of moving one red counter by three squares (not necessarily in a straight line), where from a square one may only move to a square sharing a side with it;

Jüri's move consists of moving the blue counter in the same way by one square;

the game starts with Jaak's move and ends when, at the end of Jaak's move, some red counter is on the same square as the blue counter.

Find the smallest value of $n$ for which Jaak can end the game from any initial position.

The numbers $0, 1, 2, \dots, 9$ are written around a circle in some order. Prove that

a) there are three consecutive numbers on the circle whose sum is at least $15$;

b) there need not be three consecutive numbers whose sum is greater than $15$.

Call a positive integer lonely if the sum of the reciprocals of its positive divisors (including $1$ and the number itself) is not equal to the sum of the reciprocals of the positive divisors of any other positive integer. Prove that

a) all primes are lonely;

b) there are infinitely many positive integers that are not lonely.

In a lottery, a player must mark $6$ numbers out of $36$ on a ticket. In the draw, $6$ of these $36$ numbers are chosen at random, and a ticket wins if none of the drawn numbers is marked on it. Prove that

a) it is possible to mark numbers on $9$ tickets so that at least one of these tickets certainly wins in the draw;

b) it is not possible to mark numbers on $8$ tickets so that at least one of these tickets certainly wins in the draw.

Let $n$ and $c$ be coprime positive integers. For any integer $i$, let $i'$ denote the remainder of $ci$ upon division by $n$. Let $A_0A_1 \ldots A_{n-1}$ be a regular $n$-gon. Prove that

a) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are parallel, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also parallel;

b) if the segments $A_iA_j$ and $A_kA_l$ are perpendicular, then the segments $A_{i'}A_{j'}$ and $A_{k'}A_{l'}$ are also perpendicular.

A sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots$ is called periodic modulo $n$ if there exists a positive integer $k$ such that for every positive integer $i$ the terms $a_i$ and $a_{i+k}$ leave the same remainder when divided by $n$. Does there exist an increasing sequence of positive integers which

a) is not periodic modulo a finite positive number of positive integer moduli and is periodic modulo all the other positive integer moduli;

b) is not periodic modulo infinitely many positive integer moduli and is periodic modulo infinitely many positive integer moduli?

Does there exist an equilateral triangle all of whose vertices have integer coordinates

a) in the plane;

b) in space?

All positive integers less than the positive integer $n$ and coprime to it are added in increasing order. How many times, after adding the next number, does a partial sum divisible by $n$ occur, if

a) $n$ is an odd prime;

b) $n$ is the square of an odd prime?

Does there exist a geometric sequence whose terms include

a) $3$, $45$ and $2025$;

b) $3$, $\frac{45}{\sqrt[3]{5}}$ and $2025$?
